WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.738
Welcome back to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.688 --> 00:00:07.800
This is Chapter Six, Episode Eight: the Faroe Islands.

3
00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:12.700
Færøernes geografi, the geography of the Faroe Islands.

4
00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:21.825
The Faroe Islands are a group of islands consisting of 18 islands, which lie in the Atlantic Ocean between Scotland, Iceland and Norway.

5
00:00:21.825 --> 00:00:28.482
The islands' total area of 1,400 square kilometres corresponds roughly to that of Lolland.

6
00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:32.607
The Faroe Islands have 55,000 inhabitants.

7
00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:39.084
About 14,000 of them live in the capital Tórshavn, which is also the largest town in the Faroe Islands.

8
00:00:39.634 --> 00:00:43.534
The landscape is marked by steep, grass-covered cliffs.

9
00:00:43.484 --> 00:00:49.254
The highest point, Slættaratindur, lies 880 metres above sea level.

10
00:00:49.804 --> 00:00:56.529
The Faroe Islands have a climate with mild winters and cool summers as well as large amounts of rain.

11
00:00:56.479 --> 00:01:02.729
The winter in Tórshavn is thus, in spite of the northern location, a little warmer than in Denmark.

12
00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:06.279
The rest of the year it is however somewhat cooler than in Denmark.

13
00:01:06.279 --> 00:01:13.944
The weather often changes quickly, and despite the small size of the country there is often a big difference in the weather from island to island.

14
00:01:14.494 --> 00:01:16.957
The history of the Faroe Islands.

15
00:01:16.907 --> 00:01:23.194
The Faroe Islands were first populated in the 600s by Irish monks who settled on the islands.

16
00:01:23.194 --> 00:01:31.519
The monks were however quickly displaced by Vikings from primarily Norway, who carried out an extensive settlement of the Faroe Islands.

17
00:01:31.519 --> 00:01:37.218
Thereby the Faroe Islands became part of the North Atlantic cultural and trading community.

18
00:01:37.768 --> 00:01:46.932
During the Middle Ages the Faroe Islands came under the Norwegian king and thus followed in 1380 with Norway into the union with Denmark.

19
00:01:47.482 --> 00:01:52.582
The Faroe Islands got their name from the Old Norse word fær, which means sheep.

20
00:01:52.532 --> 00:02:00.607
That refers to the many sheep that are raised on the islands, and which up to the 1800s were the Faroe Islands' most important industry.

21
00:02:00.607 --> 00:02:06.557
And wool and clothes made of wool were the islands' by far biggest trade goods with foreign countries.

22
00:02:06.557 --> 00:02:10.982
The Danish king gradually got direct control of the trade on the islands.

23
00:02:10.982 --> 00:02:20.307
The Faroese could only sell their goods at fixed prices to Danish merchants, and from 1708 only to the Royal Monopoly Trade in Tórshavn.

24
00:02:20.307 --> 00:02:30.332
Conversely, Denmark had a duty to supply the Faroese population with necessary goods from outside, for example grain, timber, iron and salt.

25
00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:37.932
Denmark could however not always protect the islands, which therefore often got visits from foreign smugglers and pirates.

26
00:02:37.932 --> 00:02:49.350
Among other things, the islands were in 1629 attacked by North African pirates, who killed six persons and carried off and sold about 30 as slaves in North Africa.

27
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:59.225
The Faroe Islands came under the first Danish constitution in 1849 and were thereby also represented in the Folketing and the Landsting.

28
00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:09.600
Trade with foreign countries was made free in 1856, and from the end of the 1800s the Faroe Islands developed into a modern, international fishing nation.

29
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.880
It was especially the large cod fishing that now became the islands' most important trade good.

30
00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:30.367
Since the Reformation in the 1500s, the church, the administration and the school had been completely dominated by the Danish language, while Faroese for centuries survived especially as an oral language through a rich tradition of folk songs.

31
00:03:30.317 --> 00:03:46.314
Only in the middle of the 1800s was an official Faroese written language created, which, together with a rising national awareness, was decisive for the Faroese language surviving and for it today being the main language on the islands rather than Danish.

32
00:03:46.864 --> 00:03:54.314
When Denmark in 1940 was occupied by the Germans, the Faroe Islands were occupied by Great Britain.

33
00:03:54.264 --> 00:04:04.239
The islands had great strategic importance for the transport routes between the USA and Europe, and therefore the British wanted to prevent Germany from occupying the islands.

34
00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:13.801
Great Britain did not interfere further in Faroese politics, and therefore it became possible for the Faroese to govern more themselves than they had been used to under Denmark.

35
00:04:13.801 --> 00:04:24.348
When Great Britain after the end of the war in 1945 gave the Faroe Islands back to Denmark again, the wish for independence on the islands had grown considerably.

36
00:04:24.898 --> 00:04:31.523
After the Second World War, negotiations began about a home rule arrangement for the Faroe Islands.

37
00:04:31.473 --> 00:04:37.798
A referendum in the Faroe Islands however showed a very small majority for independence from Denmark.

38
00:04:37.798 --> 00:04:44.635
Most politicians in the Løgting, as well as the Danish government, did not regard the result as clear.

39
00:04:44.635 --> 00:04:56.560
After a new election to the Løgting, agreement was reached between the Faroe Islands and Denmark about home rule for the Faroe Islands, which therefore remained in the realm community with Denmark.

40
00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.298
The Faroe Islands got home rule by law in 1948.

41
00:05:00.298 --> 00:05:11.485
The home rule consists of the Løgting, which is an elected and legislative assembly, and an administration led by a government, a landsstyre, that is the political leadership.

42
00:05:11.485 --> 00:05:16.548
The Løgting can have up to 33 members, elected for four years at a time.

43
00:05:16.548 --> 00:05:20.848
The chair of the government, the lagmand, is elected by the Løgting.

44
00:05:20.848 --> 00:05:25.518
The Løgting passes the laws in all areas the home rule has taken over.

45
00:05:26.068 --> 00:05:39.630
These include, among other things, social services, education, tax, housing, fishing, raw materials in the subsoil, municipal matters, culture and leisure, and infrastructure.

46
00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.800
In 2005 the Faroese home rule arrangement was expanded.

47
00:05:44.350 --> 00:05:57.887
The Faroe Islands can today take over all areas, except the state constitution, citizenship, the Supreme Court, foreign, defence and security policy, and currency and monetary policy.

48
00:05:57.837 --> 00:06:06.700
The Faroe Islands receive in 2025 an economic grant from the Danish state of about 575 million kroner a year.

49
00:06:06.700 --> 00:06:15.962
Fishing is by far the most important export income source for Faroese society, but also shipping and tourism have great importance for the economy.

50
00:06:15.962 --> 00:06:24.050
When Denmark became a member of the EF, the EU, in 1973, the Faroe Islands chose to stay outside.

51
00:06:24.050 --> 00:06:29.058
That also means the Faroe Islands can enter their own trade agreements outside the EU.

52
00:06:29.608 --> 00:06:35.620
Christianity generally plays a bigger role in social life in the Faroe Islands than in Denmark.

53
00:06:35.570 --> 00:06:38.870
There are still many Faroese who go to church on Sundays.

54
00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:50.658
While Denmark introduced free abortion in 1973, and a large majority of the Danish population supports free abortion, there is greater disagreement on the issue in the Faroe Islands.

55
00:06:50.658 --> 00:06:56.020
Only in 2025 did the Løgting adopt introducing free abortion on the islands.

56
00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.745
It applies up to the 12th week of pregnancy.

57
00:06:58.745 --> 00:07:06.245
17 members of the Løgting voted for and 16 against, and thereby there was only just exactly a majority for it.

58
00:07:06.245 --> 00:07:14.028
Until now the law has only allowed abortion if the woman has been raped, or if the pregnancy threatens her life and health.

59
00:07:14.578 --> 00:07:16.603
Faroese cultural life.

60
00:07:16.553 --> 00:07:19.515
The Faroe Islands have a rich cultural life.

61
00:07:19.515 --> 00:07:34.374
Authors such as the two cousins Jørgen-Frantz Jacobsen, who lived from 1900 to 38, and William Heinesen, 1900 to 91, are known both in the Faroe Islands, in Denmark and in several places abroad.

62
00:07:34.924 --> 00:07:42.174
Jørgen-Frantz Jacobsen's main work is Barbara, which came out in 1939, the year after his death.

63
00:07:42.124 --> 00:07:45.999
The novel is set in the 1700s in the Faroe Islands.

64
00:07:45.999 --> 00:07:51.686
Barbara is a younger woman who falls in love easily and also has great attraction for men.

65
00:07:51.686 --> 00:07:56.699
Everyone in the small, religious local community has an opinion about Barbara.

66
00:07:56.699 --> 00:08:06.774
It is told, among other things, that it is her "sin" that is to blame for the death of her two earlier husbands, two former priests, and for having seduced the new priest.

67
00:08:06.774 --> 00:08:17.544
Like the Faroe Islands' wild nature and harsh and changeable weather conditions, Barbara's nature also cannot be tamed and has a great effect on the local community.

68
00:08:18.094 --> 00:08:33.919
The theme of social deviance, that is when people's way of life does not fit into a small local community with narrow limits for right and wrong, also recurs in William Heinesen's main work De fortabte spillemænd, "The Lost Musicians", from 1950.

69
00:08:33.869 --> 00:08:40.056
At the centre of the story is a group of musicians who live a life with music, alcohol and women.

70
00:08:40.056 --> 00:08:52.650
The hardworking, thrifty and religious local population has difficulty accepting this "dissolute" way of life, and therefore the musicians cannot fit in and gain a foothold and stability in life.

71
00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:60.000
In recent years the poet Vónbjørt Vang, born 1974, has made herself noticed.

72
00:08:59.950 --> 00:09:17.772
In 2025 she received the Nordic Council's Literature Prize for the poetry collection Svørt Okidé, "Black Orchid", which is about a mother's fear of losing her teenage son, who has disappeared into abuse of alcohol and narcotics and later into the psychiatric system.

73
00:09:18.322 --> 00:09:21.709
Also Faroese painting is at a very high level.

74
00:09:21.659 --> 00:09:28.584
Faroese painters often paint Faroese motifs such as the wild nature and life in the small villages.

75
00:09:28.584 --> 00:09:35.022
But in a way and with a choice of colours which raises the Faroese up to something that all people can recognise.

76
00:09:35.572 --> 00:09:39.235
The Faroe Islands' flag is called Merkið, "the Mark".

77
00:09:39.184 --> 00:09:47.034
It was drawn up in 1919 and is a flag of the Nordic type with a cross and with the colours white, blue and red.

78
00:09:47.034 --> 00:10:01.659
Since Denmark had been occupied by Germany in 1940, and the Faroe Islands by Great Britain, the British demanded that it should be possible to distinguish between Danish and Faroese ships, which until then had also used Dannebrog.

79
00:10:01.659 --> 00:10:08.647
Therefore Great Britain approved Merkið as the Faroe Islands' flag on the 25th of April 1940.

80
00:10:08.647 --> 00:10:14.972
Every year on the 25th of April the Faroese gather for public meetings to celebrate their flag.

81
00:10:14.972 --> 00:10:18.864
You typically hear a flag speech and sing community songs.

82
00:10:19.414 --> 00:10:27.514
The flag is also seen in every town in the Faroe Islands on the 29th of July, which is the Faroese national day.

83
00:10:27.464 --> 00:10:39.614
On this day the Ólavsøka, the Olaf Festival, is held, which is named after Olaf the Holy, who was king of Norway and died in a battle on the 29th of July 1030.

84
00:10:39.614 --> 00:10:51.642
Most of the Faroese of that time were descendants of Norwegian emigrants, and therefore Olaf the Holy has become the Faroe Islands' patron saint, that is the saint who is said to protect the Faroe Islands.

85
00:10:52.192 --> 00:10:54.405
Here is your recap for Episode Eight.

86
00:10:54.355 --> 00:11:06.405
The Faroe Islands have 18 islands in the Atlantic Ocean between Scotland, Iceland and Norway, an area of 1,400 square kilometres, similar to Lolland.

87
00:11:06.405 --> 00:11:11.292
There are 55,000 inhabitants, about 14,000 in Tórshavn.

88
00:11:11.292 --> 00:11:15.980
The highest point is Slættaratindur, 880 metres.

89
00:11:15.980 --> 00:11:20.455
Irish monks arrived in the 600s, and then Vikings from Norway.

90
00:11:20.455 --> 00:11:25.042
The islands followed Norway into the union with Denmark in 1380.

91
00:11:25.042 --> 00:11:28.942
The name comes from the Old Norse word fær, meaning sheep.

92
00:11:28.942 --> 00:11:35.267
The Royal Monopoly Trade existed from 1708; trade was freed in 1856.

93
00:11:35.267 --> 00:11:39.830
The Faroese written language was created in the middle of the 1800s.

94
00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:45.980
Great Britain occupied the Faroe Islands in 1940, and gave them back in 1945.

95
00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:51.317
Home rule by law came in 1948 and was expanded in 2005.

96
00:11:51.317 --> 00:11:56.617
The Løgting can have up to 33 members, and the chair of the government is the lagmand.

97
00:11:56.617 --> 00:12:02.505
The Danish grant in 2025 is about 575 million kroner.

98
00:12:02.505 --> 00:12:06.655
The Faroe Islands stayed outside the EF in 1973.

99
00:12:06.655 --> 00:12:10.855
Today neither Greenland nor the Faroe Islands is part of the EU.

100
00:12:10.855 --> 00:12:22.692
Exams round the populations: the Faroe Islands, 55,000, may appear as about 50,000, and Greenland, 57,000, as about 55,000.

101
00:12:22.692 --> 00:12:29.017
Free abortion up to week 12 was adopted in 2025, by 17 votes to 16.

102
00:12:29.017 --> 00:12:38.342
Authors: Jørgen-Frantz Jacobsen wrote Barbara, 1939, and William Heinesen wrote De fortabte spillemænd, 1950.

103
00:12:38.342 --> 00:12:43.067
Vónbjørt Vang won the Nordic Council Literature Prize in 2025.

104
00:12:43.067 --> 00:12:50.742
The flag, Merkið, was approved by Great Britain on the 25th of April 1940, which is Flag Day.

105
00:12:50.742 --> 00:12:58.500
The Faroese national day is the 29th of July, with the Ólavsøka festival, named after Olaf the Holy.

106
00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.650
Now it is time for your exam practice.

107
00:13:01.600 --> 00:13:06.513
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

108
00:13:06.513 --> 00:13:13.998
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

109
00:13:14.548 --> 00:13:15.798
Question 1.

110
00:13:15.748 --> 00:13:17.773
When was free abortion introduced?

111
00:13:17.773 --> 00:13:20.511
Option A: 1935.

112
00:13:20.511 --> 00:13:23.173
Option B: 1951.

113
00:13:23.173 --> 00:13:25.944
Option C: 1973.
[DA] Hvornår blev der indført fri abort?  A: 1935  |  B: 1951  |  C: 1973

114
00:13:32.494 --> 00:13:35.382
The answer is C: 1973.

115
00:13:35.332 --> 00:13:39.594
It appeared in the November 2020 and June 2022 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: 1973

116
00:13:40.144 --> 00:13:41.394
Question 2.

117
00:13:41.344 --> 00:13:44.269
How many inhabitants do the Faroe Islands have?

118
00:13:44.269 --> 00:13:46.294
The exams round the figure.

119
00:13:46.294 --> 00:13:49.269
Option A: About 55,000.

120
00:13:49.269 --> 00:13:53.057
Option B: About 155,000.

121
00:13:53.057 --> 00:13:56.580
Option C: About 255,000.
[DA] Hvor mange indbyggere har Færøerne?  A: Cirka 55.000  |  B: Cirka 155.000  |  C: Cirka 255.000

122
00:14:03.130 --> 00:14:06.180
The answer is A: About 55,000.

123
00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:09.030
It appeared in the June 2026 exam.
[DA] ✔ A: Cirka 55.000

124
00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:10.918
Question 3.

125
00:14:10.868 --> 00:14:13.830
What is the largest town in the Faroe Islands called?

126
00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:15.893
Option A: Reykjavik.

127
00:14:15.893 --> 00:14:17.493
Option B: Nuuk.

128
00:14:17.493 --> 00:14:19.656
Option C: Tórshavn.
[DA] Hvad hedder Færøernes største by?  A: Reykjavik  |  B: Nuuk  |  C: Tórshavn

129
00:14:26.206 --> 00:14:28.494
The answer is C: Tórshavn.

130
00:14:28.444 --> 00:14:33.114
It appeared in the November 2022 and November 2024 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Tórshavn

131
00:14:33.664 --> 00:14:34.964
Question 4.

132
00:14:34.914 --> 00:14:37.802
Which symbol is part of the Faroe Islands' flag?

133
00:14:37.802 --> 00:14:39.877
Option A: A circle.

134
00:14:39.877 --> 00:14:42.089
Option B: A star.

135
00:14:42.089 --> 00:14:44.460
Option C: A cross.
[DA] Hvilket symbol indgår i Færøernes flag?  A: En cirkel  |  B: En stjerne  |  C: Et kors

136
00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:53.373
The answer is C: A cross.

137
00:14:53.323 --> 00:14:57.486
It appeared in the May 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ C: Et kors

138
00:14:58.036 --> 00:14:59.799
That's the end of this part.

139
00:14:59.749 --> 00:15:00.816
Well done.

